\import{base-macros}

\p{\strong{Status:} implemented. Two commands carry it: the machine's own gate for one switch set, and the end-to-end guard of a distribution. Since 2026-11-24 the hostile exports are a step of the guard and are judged by the official verdict and by their kind. Revised on 2026-11-24 for the state at the end of that day: the hostile suite has 1,196 rows, the guard also runs through the executable, and it has passed on the distribution in which the machine is the default engine. Revised on 2026-11-25: the suite has 1,274 rows; and since the default configuration takes a rejection of the machine as final (\commit{a8dc6dd}, see [the decision](dec-PUU0)), the hostile step has a third count beside the agreements and the tolerated accepts, the \em{known wrong rejects}, with a mark in the manifest and a ceiling that may only go down.}

\p{A change of the machine that can change what is computed (a new [switch](design-switches), a default set, a fix) is published only with evidence that no verdict moved. The gate is that evidence in a fixed form, so that two runs can be compared and nobody has to trust a hand-picked sample. It has five kinds of test, and each sees something the others cannot.}

\subtree[design-gate-machine]{
\title{The machine's gate for one switch set}

\p{One command of the gate tooling of \code{rlvm-machine} (\code{\verb~|report.sh gate --switches LIST~}; no list is the generic tier, called v0) measures the tree as published and writes a one-page report. Its parts:}

\ol{
\li{\strong{Differential.} Every declaration is checked by the machine and by the [reference engine](design-reference-engine), on the ladder of corpora: the tutorial tests, the \code{bugs} tests, all 230 published arena tests, Init, Std, reachability and a slice of Cedar. A unit the machine accepts and the reference engine rejects counts as SOUNDNESS, the opposite as COMPLETENESS; both must be 0. A unit the machine declines is not compared, because the reference engine decides it in a real run. A unit the reference engine itself declines is settled by the official verdict of the file.}
\li{\strong{Tier equivalence.} The generic tier must give the same outcome for every declaration as the set under test. For a set with code objects, the values of unfolding, weak-head normalisation and type inference and the answers of conversion are compared on generated applications of every constant of the tutorial tests and Init. See [code objects and tiers](design-code-objects-and-tiers).}
\li{\strong{Convention equivalence.} Host depth bound 0 and no bound are compared with the bound in force, 128: outcome, reason, ticks, call events by operation, the trace of frames, and after an accept every word of every record, every run word and every pair entry. This is the test that [control convention H](design-control-convention-h) does not change the transition system.}
\li{\strong{Audits.} Two more lanes run with the debug audits on, \code{\verb~|poison,scopeaudit,maskaudit~} and \code{\verb~|memocheck,shortcheck~}: the same outcomes and no audit failure. The second lane recomputes memo hits and re-derives every positive answer of a shortcut by the complete procedure.}
\li{\strong{Pipeline.} The real driver with \code{\verb~|-X engine=machine~} runs the 230 tests with one worker and the corpora with four: the verdict, the machine's declines, and the driver's counters for a unit that the machine rejects and the reference engine accepts, for a strict accept after a warm reject, and for disagreements. Since 2026-11-25 this step and the mutant campaign below run with \code{\verb~|-X verify=reference~}: only the re-check of a rejection fills these counters, and the campaign compares the final verdict with the reference pipeline's, which has the re-check.}
}

\p{A wrong verdict, a difference, a crash or a timeout fails the gate. Declines alone give "pass with declines". The lanes run as passes, one engine at a time, and are compared from digests recorded per unit, so the peak memory is one engine on one declaration.}
}

\subtree[design-gate-mutants]{
\title{Mutants, through the real driver}

\p{The test kit keeps four corpora of mutated exports, 17,775 files in all, each classified once by the official checker: 12,260 mutants of the tutorial tests, 2,692 of the \code{bugs}, \code{other} and \code{corner-cases} tests, 847 of the prelude, and 1,976 that wrap one expression in a metadata record. The mutant command runs every file through the real pipeline per switch set and compares the final verdict with the stored official one, with the same pipeline on the reference engine, and, per declaration and in process, the machine's own answer with the reference engine's. The last comparison matters because the driver trusts an accept of the machine. For v0 and for the default set of the wave-6 integration: SOUNDNESS 0 and COMPLETENESS 0 on 17,775 mutants per set, 16,092 in agreement and 1,683 declined, which is exactly what the reference pipeline declines; over 1,008,568 declarations per set the machine never accepts what the reference engine rejects, nor the reverse.}
}

\subtree[design-gate-diff]{
\title{The differential engine through the launcher}

\p{\code{\verb~|-X engine=diff~} of \code{rlvm-testkit} is an engine that runs the engine under test and the reference kernel on every unit inside a normal run, with reader, loader, environment pass, driver and workers. It is the form used on the large corpora. Its self-test is a deliberately broken engine with eight kinds of lies that the run must catch.}
}

\subtree[design-gate-hostile]{
\title{The hostile suite, with kinds}

\p{Hostile exports are small hand-built files, each attacking one shortcut or memo; see [the adversarial review method](design-adversarial-review). The suite lives with the machine's tests as compressed exports and a manifest with four columns: name, the verdict of the official checker of Lean 4.36.0, marks, and what the export attacks. It started as the 329 exports of the first soundness review and had 421 rows in the afternoon of 2026-11-24, 85 that the official checker accepts and 336 that it rejects. That evening the rows of every review entered it, each with its official verdict taken again from the official checker: the 423 of the review of the default set, 279 of the review of the shortcuts, 41 of the prototype on exact proof conversion and 25 of the audit of the reference engine, 1,189 rows; and then the seven metadata exports of [finding R2-F4](ex-FD6K), \strong{1,196 rows}.}

\p{The rule of a row follows the [exactness contract](design-exactness-contract). A row with the mark \code{T} is of kind T: its statement is derivable and the official checker rejects it only through its own non-transitivity, cache order or object identity; such a row demands the official verdict or an accept. Six of the 421 rows carried the mark; of the 1,196 rows 83 do, each with the reviewer and the reason in the manifest. Every other row is strict and demands the official verdict. Further marks say that a decline is by design: among the 421 rows, 46 enter eager reduction and are declined without the switch \code{eager}, and 18 of them are declined even with it. Three notes were added with the larger suite: 11 rows that the official checker accepts and the machine's first lane may reject through its cache history, where the pipeline's re-check gives the official accept and a rejection without the note fails the suite; the rows with a metadata node that hides something from an official test, whose rule is the official verdict or a decline through the launcher; and rows that the reference engine declines in process.}

\p{\strong{The mark \code{wrong-reject} and the ceiling}, since 2026-11-25. The first of the three notes, \code{recheck}, said that the pipeline repairs the row. Since a plain run takes a rejection of the machine as final, it no longer does: of the 13 rows with the note, the default configuration rejects twelve (the thirteenth is rejected only with the switch \code{k2}, which is in no default set). The twelve carry the mark \code{wrong-reject} beside the note, each with its cause in the text of the row, and the header of the manifest explains the mark. It is the counterpart of the mark \code{T} for the other direction, with one difference: a tolerated accept is allowed by the contract, a known wrong reject is a debt. \src{scripts/gate-hostile.py} judges a rejection of an official accept on a marked row as "known wrong reject" and on any other row as a wrong reject, which fails the gate as before. It prints every known wrong reject by name on every run, and \src{scripts/gate.sh} repeats the lines, so the count is never only a number. The number may only go down: \src{scripts/gate.sh} carries it as a constant, 12, and passes it as a ceiling, so a new wrong reject is not admitted by a mark alone and a thirteenth fails the gate; a marked row that gets the official verdict is listed as "no longer rejected", and then its mark goes and the constant goes down with it. The twelve rows and what would close them are in [accepted risks](accepted-risks-wrong-rejects). In process the suite judges the machine's own answers and is unchanged; its words "repaired by the re-check" for the rows with the note now describe \code{\verb~|-X verify=reference~}.}

\p{The suite runs in process as a fast test group at every publication, as a slow matrix of every row under some thirty switch sets (32 sets by 329 rows, 10,528 judgments, for the default set of the wave-6 integration; 33 sets by 419 rows, 13,827 judgments, at the publication of the switch \code{eager}; 56 sets by 1,196 rows for [the default set of the evening of 2026-11-24](log-HS1E)), and through the launcher as a step of the guard below.}
}

\subtree[design-gate-guard]{
\title{The end-to-end guard of a distribution}

\p{\src{scripts/gate.sh} is what every owner runs before publishing a behavioural change: build the distribution; all 230 arena tests through the JVM launcher with no wrong verdict and no decline; the mutant corpora with SOUNDNESS 0 and no good mutant rejected; the self-test of the differential path; and, with \code{\verb~|--hostile~}, the hostile rows through the launcher. The oracle of every step is an official verdict and never the opinion of one of the project's engines, so the same command gates a distribution whose default engine is the reference engine and one whose default engine is the machine. \code{\verb~|--defaults~} runs the distribution exactly as a user runs it, with automatic workers, the default engine and the default load mode; it is the gate of a change of a default. Since 2026-11-25 that configuration takes a rejection of the machine as final; \code{\verb~|scripts/gate.sh --defaults -X verify=reference~} gates the verified configuration that was the default before, and without \code{\verb~|--defaults~} the gate runs the reference engine and is unchanged. \code{\verb~|--native~} runs the tests, the mutants and the hostile rows through the executable instead of the jars on a JVM: the whole guard then takes three minutes, the 17,775 mutants 64 s against 46 minutes, with the same counts.}

\p{\strong{The guard on the machine-default distribution}, 2026-11-24, with no option anywhere: 230 of 230 published tests correct and none declined; 17,775 mutants with SOUNDNESS 0 and COMPLETENESS 0, 15,741 in agreement and 2,034 declined; the self-test of the differential path; the hostile suite of 1,196 rows with 1,124 in agreement, 68 tolerated accepts, 4 declined, no wrong accept and no wrong reject. It passed with these counts three times: on the distribution of the integrator before the machine was published as default engine, and on the distribution the team shares, built from the frozen set of jars, through the executable and through the jars. The 2,034 declines of the mutants are the 1,683 of before and 351 correct rejections of mutants with a metadata record that [the launcher's safety net](log-TRJ0) reports as declines. With the reference engine as default engine the hostile step had 6 tolerated accepts and 4 declines on 1,189 rows. See [the decision on the default engine](dec-T79V).}

\p{\strong{The guard since the change of 2026-11-25}, with no option anywhere, through the executable of the published build: 230 of 230 published tests correct and none declined; 17,775 mutants with SOUNDNESS 0 and COMPLETENESS 0, 16,092 in agreement and 1,683 declined, because the 351 correct rejections that the launcher's safety net reported as declines are rejections again; the hostile suite of 1,274 rows with 1,184 in agreement, 74 tolerated accepts, 12 known wrong rejects (12 marked, at most 12 allowed), 4 declined, no wrong accept and no other wrong reject. The summary line of the hostile step reads:}

\pre{\verb~|
hostile: 1274 rows: agree 1184, kind-T accepts tolerated 74, KNOWN WRONG REJECT 12 (12 marked, at most 12), WRONG ACCEPT 0, WRONG REJECT 0, declined 4, errors 0
~}

\p{The build of the patch had passed with the same counts through the executable and through the jars before it was published, the self-test of the differential path included. The same gate with \code{\verb~|-X verify=reference~} gives the counts of the build before the change, on which the suite already had 1,274 rows: 15,741 mutants in agreement and 2,034 declined; on the hostile suite 1,192 in agreement, 78 tolerated accepts, 4 declined, no known wrong reject, and the 12 marked rows listed as no longer rejected. Against that, a plain run has 8 agreements fewer on the hostile suite: 12 official accepts are rejected, and 4 official rejections that the re-check had turned into tolerated accepts get the official verdict. See [the census](rep-MO-1), section 8.}
}

\subtree[design-gate-limits]{
\title{What the gate could not see, and what it taught}

\p{\strong{A differential against the reference engine is blind where both engines agree with each other and not with the official checker, and where real input never goes.} The default set of the wave-6 integration passed all of the above, including the differential engine with 0 differences on 760,839 units of eight corpora, and the machine was made the default engine. Within the hour, 67 new hostile exports with official verdicts showed five wrong accepts, three of them in v0: a bound variable was typed by what its slot holds, and such a type is only convertible with the declared one. On real corpora and on mutants the two types are the same, and the 329 rows of the suite had no row of that shape. See [the decision on the default engine](dec-T79V) and [the rule that followed](dec-QN1O).}

\p{\strong{Lanes over one store share state that a real run does not.} Two pieces of state live in the store and not in the engine: the bits that record that a closed term passed check mode, and the hints that tell a binder node which code block was built for it. The gate runs up to six engines over one store. An audit lane that elaborates in another order wrote hints that turned block activations of the compared lanes into walks: the gate of the default set reported between 270 and 45,821 "different" declarations on Std and the Cedar slice, never the same number twice, in ticks only and never in outcomes. Every lane now has a table of its own for the bits, and the compared lanes run before the others. A real run has one machine engine per store and is not affected.}
}

\p{Detail: [the working notes of package M8](rep-machine-M8) (commands, deviations, every run), \src{rlvm-testkit/README.md} (the differential engine, the mutant corpora, the official-verdict cache), [the notes of the hostile suite](rep-machine-S) (the suite and its tests), [the switch reference](design-switches-document) (the gates of the default set), [the integration notes of the machine](rep-machine-INT) (the two harness findings), [the architecture document](design-architecture-document) section 9. Source: [the machine session](ses-Z72N), [the wave-6 session](ses-CVTE), [the exactness session](ses-874D) and [the finalization session](ses-A14B).}

